// LEARNING-RECORD §6 — a classification policy must be PARAMETRIC, and the
// parametric instrument REVEALS policy collisions that a hardcoded check
// hides. Class-level problem found while implementing the Socratic
// document-tree policy as code (rmw3 directive: "esto debe ser código,
// instrumentos que se puedan escalar — no soluciones particulares").

learning_record("20260817_classification_policy_parametric") {
  uuid:      "c8d2e4f6-9a3b-4c5d-8e7f-0a1b2c3d4e5f",
  parent:    "20260817_s2_instrument_census_bidirectional.vsm (audit trail)",
  family:    "policy-instrumentation",
  class:     "verification-gap",
  severity:  "high",
  detection: "S3* audit 2026-08-17: classification_policy_plan.vsm PART IV
             DECLARED 'the tree is READ from this plan doc, parametric,
             never hard-coded in the check' but checks/classification-valid
             hard-coded home_of_type in 186 lines of bash case statements.
             The policy contradicted its own instrument. Rebuilding the
             check as a DELEGATE to classify.py (which reads tree_rules from
             the policy) made the instrument reveal 4 REAL violations (the
             axis2_*/EXP-* experiment specs living in spec/ when the tree
             classifies them sandbox/) AND a policy collision: dir_schema
             .spec.vsm still declared those types under spec/ while the tree
             declared sandbox/. The hardcoded check had accepted both.",
  root_cause: "the check was the SECOND home of the policy (the tree was
             duplicated in bash). Two copies of a policy always drift; the
             second copy becomes the enforcement and the FIRST copy becomes
             decoration. The parametric instrument (policy as data, check as
             interpreter) has ONE copy: change the policy and every gate
             re-classifies without editing any check.",
  evidence:  "classification-valid pass → 'PASS: 90 documentos en directorio
             canónico (árbol paramétrico)' EXIT=0; classify.py --self →
             'PASS: 14 rules loaded'; dir-schema-valid → clean after the
             policy was updated to match the tree; before the fix the two
             checks DISAGREED (classification-valid FAIL on 4 docs that
             dir-schema-valid accepted).",
  lesson:    "1) A parametric instrument is not a preference — it is the only
             form that cannot drift: the policy lives in ONE place and every
             consumer re-reads it. 2) The order of declarative rules IS
             semantic: fnmatch first-match-wins means 'DESIGN before PLAN'
             decides plan_tree_design → design-sources/; broad globs
             (*BOOT-*, *VSL-*, *THEORY-*) create false positives that force
             precise patterns (*S1-*-CONTRACT*, *VSF-INSTANCE-v* vs LAYER).
             3) When two policies disagree (tree vs dir_schema), the
             instrument surfaces it as a RED gate — the resolution is to
             update the STALE policy to match the CANON, never to loosen the
             check (R4: the check is the truth witness). 4) A check that
             returns EXIT=0 on everything is not a gate — it is a rubber
             stamp; R4.1 fail-mode must always exit non-zero.",
  action:    "checks/classification-valid DELEGATES to scripts/vsl/classify.py
             (parametric, modes pass/check-file/fail); tree_rules table (14
             rules) declared in classification_policy_plan.vsm PART IV-B;
             dir_schema.spec.vsm updated to match the tree; spec/ reduced to
             readme-only residual; 4 experiment specs relocated spec/ →
             sandbox/ per PART II NODO 3 (no active parent plan → sandbox/
             until attached); agent_map.vsm registers classify.py +
             classification-valid (9 instruments, all with design).",
  propagation: "Every future policy gate should follow this shape: policy
             declares a machine-readable table (data), a script INTERPRETS
             it, the check DELEGATES to the script (never re-implements the
             table), fail-mode always exits non-zero, and a change to the
             policy re-classifies without touching the check. Same pattern
             as write_gates.vsm + orchestrator, and version-change-plan:
             canonical read, parametric checks.",
  organ:     "S5 (policy) → S3* (audit) → S2 (map) → R4.1 (checks) → pre-commit (gates)",
  recursion: "root system, document layer (spec_revision/)",
  canonical: "docs/spec_revision/plan/classification-policy-plan-v1/classification_policy_plan.vsm (PART II/IV-B)",
  evidence:  "classification-valid pass → 'PASS: 90 documentos en directorio
             canónico (árbol paramétrico)' EXIT=0; classify.py --self →
             'PASS: 14 rules loaded'; dir-schema-valid → clean (policy
             updated to match tree); pre-commit full: EXIT=0",
  action:    "When a policy change is declared, ALWAYS re-run the full gate
             chain (classification-valid, dir-schema-valid, agent-map-valid,
             design-sources-index-valid) — the parametric instrument
             re-classifies the whole corpus in one pass; a red gate means a
             policy collision to resolve at the CANON level (update the
             stale policy), never by loosening the check.",
